Let a plain getline read on through all the input files, as POSIX
specifies, rather than stopping at the end of the current one.
"getline" and "getline var" returned 0 at the end of each input file,
and a loop such as "while ((getline) > 0)" saw only the rest of that
file. Once the input was used up, as in an END rule, they found no file
to open and returned -1. Now getline goes on to the next file at the
end of each one, and returns 0 when there is no more input. The main
loop checks for input used up by getline in an action, and FNR is reset
as each file is opened rather than at the top of the main loop, so a
record read by getline in BEGIN or across a file boundary counts toward
FNR of the file it came from. Since getline in BEGIN or in an action
can now finish reading standard input, moving past it no longer closes
stdin, which stays open as "/dev/stdin": a later getline from
"/dev/stdin" read a closed stream rather than returning 0.

